WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.350
Here's a meeting you might recognize. One colleague wants

2
00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:06.090
a draft to read first. Another wants to talk

3
00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:09.890
before anyone writes. A manager needs a recommendation, and

4
00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:12.790
a partner wants time to consult their team. Who's

5
00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:14.310
approaching the work the right way?

6
00:00:14.818 --> 00:00:17.778
I'd say we need more information. Each approach could

7
00:00:17.798 --> 00:00:21.038
help. The difficulty starts when my preferred way quietly

8
00:00:21.058 --> 00:00:22.998
becomes the rule for everyone else.

9
00:00:23.566 --> 00:00:26.726
That's what this course invites us to notice. Cultural

10
00:00:26.746 --> 00:00:29.266
values can help us ask better questions about our

11
00:00:29.286 --> 00:00:32.446
expectations. They don't tell us what someone believes because

12
00:00:32.466 --> 00:00:35.726
of their race, background, disability, age, or role.

13
00:00:36.656 --> 00:00:39.956
So, if a partner wants to consult colleagues, I

14
00:00:39.976 --> 00:00:42.416
can ask whose input they need and how much

15
00:00:42.436 --> 00:00:45.756
time would help. I shouldn't assume they can't make

16
00:00:45.776 --> 00:00:46.376
a decision.

17
00:00:47.186 --> 00:00:50.606
Yes. That's one way to explore individual and collective

18
00:00:50.626 --> 00:00:54.606
priorities. Authority is another lens. Someone may expect a

19
00:00:54.626 --> 00:00:57.206
manager to decide and still have an important concern

20
00:00:57.226 --> 00:01:00.086
to raise. We can explain who decides and offer

21
00:01:00.106 --> 00:01:02.026
a usable way to question the information.

22
00:01:02.676 --> 00:01:06.376
A quiet meeting doesn't necessarily mean agreement. Written input

23
00:01:06.396 --> 00:01:09.076
or a private follow-up might reveal something the discussion

24
00:01:09.096 --> 00:01:09.516
missed.

25
00:01:10.566 --> 00:01:13.546
Let's return to that request for a draft. How

26
00:01:13.566 --> 00:01:16.726
might uncertainty and time horizons affect what people need?

27
00:01:17.283 --> 00:01:20.683
A draft can make the task more predictable. A

28
00:01:20.703 --> 00:01:23.963
conversation can help us discover what belongs in it.

29
00:01:24.363 --> 00:01:27.503
And while I'm thinking about Friday's deadline, a partner

30
00:01:27.523 --> 00:01:29.843
may be thinking about how the guidance will work

31
00:01:29.863 --> 00:01:31.063
for the next six months.

32
00:01:31.826 --> 00:01:34.666
Both matter. We can settle what needs a decision

33
00:01:34.686 --> 00:01:37.946
now and agree when to revisit what remains uncertain.

34
00:01:38.546 --> 00:01:40.966
We don't need to label one person resistant and

35
00:01:40.986 --> 00:01:41.966
the other adaptable.

36
00:01:42.896 --> 00:01:45.896
The course also asks us to think about cooperation,

37
00:01:46.236 --> 00:01:49.396
competition, being, and doing. What would those look like

38
00:01:49.416 --> 00:01:50.036
in this review?

39
00:01:50.922 --> 00:01:54.122
Look at what gets recognized. The final presentation may

40
00:01:54.142 --> 00:01:57.342
get attention, while careful listening or checking access needs

41
00:01:57.362 --> 00:02:00.862
goes unnoticed. We can value those contributions and still

42
00:02:00.882 --> 00:02:02.862
be clear about each person's responsibilities.

43
00:02:04.012 --> 00:02:07.492
Communication is where I often notice differences. Some people

44
00:02:07.512 --> 00:02:10.532
say the concern directly. Others expect us to understand

45
00:02:10.552 --> 00:02:12.952
it from the situation. How can we handle that

46
00:02:12.972 --> 00:02:13.452
respectfully?

47
00:02:14.142 --> 00:02:17.382
Check what you understood. You might say, I want

48
00:02:17.402 --> 00:02:19.862
to make sure I haven't missed your concern. Is

49
00:02:19.882 --> 00:02:23.702
the review time too short? Important instructions should be

50
00:02:23.722 --> 00:02:25.942
clear enough that people don't have to guess.

51
00:02:26.823 --> 00:02:30.563
And visible emotion isn't a shortcut to understanding. A

52
00:02:30.583 --> 00:02:33.403
quiet voice doesn't prove indifference, and a forceful voice

53
00:02:33.423 --> 00:02:35.623
doesn't settle whether someone's reasoning is sound.

54
00:02:36.953 --> 00:02:40.953
Exactly. Ask about the concern. Then consider common rules

55
00:02:40.973 --> 00:02:45.493
and particular circumstances. Where is flexibility available, and how

56
00:02:45.513 --> 00:02:48.933
can someone request it fairly? A personal connection shouldn't

57
00:02:48.953 --> 00:02:50.573
be the only route to extra time.

58
00:02:51.083 --> 00:02:54.143
The same applies to time and scheduling. What looks

59
00:02:54.163 --> 00:02:56.563
like a preference for overlapping tasks could be a

60
00:02:56.583 --> 00:03:00.103
workload problem. A missed meeting could involve transportation or

61
00:03:00.123 --> 00:03:03.223
caregiving. Ask about the actual constraint before calling it

62
00:03:03.243 --> 00:03:03.483
culture.

63
00:03:04.571 --> 00:03:07.651
We've touched all ten lenses in the course. Let's

64
00:03:07.671 --> 00:03:10.451
use them to improve the review: a short draft

65
00:03:10.471 --> 00:03:14.311
beforehand, time for written comments, a focused discussion, and

66
00:03:14.331 --> 00:03:16.651
a clear decision. Which part would help your team?

67
00:03:17.261 --> 00:03:20.701
I'd start by asking: would a written outline help

68
00:03:20.721 --> 00:03:23.121
you prepare, or do we need to talk something

69
00:03:23.141 --> 00:03:26.281
through first? That's a question a person can answer

70
00:03:26.301 --> 00:03:26.981
for themselves.

71
00:03:27.777 --> 00:03:30.137
Take a moment to write a question like that

72
00:03:30.157 --> 00:03:33.797
for your own work. Then check the requirements and

73
00:03:33.817 --> 00:03:37.037
agree on an arrangement. You can pause here and

74
00:03:37.057 --> 00:03:38.157
return when you're ready.

75
00:03:39.935 --> 00:03:43.235
Afterward, ask whether people could contribute and understood the

76
00:03:43.255 --> 00:03:45.935
decision. We don't have to share every preference to

77
00:03:45.955 --> 00:03:49.435
work well together. We need curiosity, clear expectations, and

78
00:03:49.455 --> 00:03:51.455
a willingness to adjust what isn't helping.
